<header class="soc-head"><p class="featured-region">Social ladder · 1428–1521 (from the Triple Alliance to the Spanish conquest)</p><h1 class="soc-title">Social Hierarchy of the Aztec Empire</h1><p class="soc-sub">From Huey Tlatoani to Enslaved</p></header>
<div class="soc-intro"><figure class="soc-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/codex-mendoza"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/codex-mendoza.jpg" alt="Codex Mendoza"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/codex-mendoza">Codex Mendoza</a><span class="soc-credit">Unknown author · Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p class="soc-lede">Aztec society, centred on the island city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, divided people first by birth into two classes: the nobles (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pipiltin">pipiltin</a>), perhaps one person in twenty, and the commoners (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macehualtin">macehualtin</a>). Law and custom marked the line. Nobles received tribute and the labour of commoners, held estates, wore fine garments forbidden to others and were schooled for war, office and the priesthood, while commoners worked the land of their calpolli and paid tribute in goods and labour. Each class had its own grades. Above all stood the huey tlatoani, ruler of Tenochtitlan and head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Triple Alliance</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlacopan">Tlacopan</a>. Long-distance merchants enjoyed privileges close to those of the lesser nobility, landless tenants worked the nobles’ estates, and at the bottom were the enslaved, whose status was not passed to their children. In a state built on war, taking captives was a commoner’s surest road upward.</p><p class="soc-facts"><span><b>Period</b> 1428–1521 (from the Triple Alliance to the Spanish conquest)</span><span><b>People</b> not known</span><span><b>Ranks</b> 7</span><span><b>Profile</b> <a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a></span></p></div>
<section class="soc-ladder-sec" aria-labelledby="soc-ladder-t"><div class="soc-ladder-head"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-ladder-t">The ladder</h2><div class="soc-scale" role="group" aria-label="How to draw the ladder"><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="0" aria-pressed="true">By rank</button><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="1" aria-pressed="false">To scale</button></div></div><ol class="soc-pyr" data-scale="0"><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:24%;--k:0"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-ruler" href="#rank-1" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n">The huey tlatoani</span><span class="soc-bar-s">One ruler</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:37%;--k:1"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility" href="#rank-2" style="--sw:5%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Nobles</span><span class="soc-bar-s">5%</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:49%;--k:2"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-religious soc-unk" href="#rank-3" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Priests</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:62%;--k:3"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists soc-unk" href="#rank-4" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Long-distance merchants and luxury craftsmen</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:75%;--k:4"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-most" href="#rank-5" style="--sw:95%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Commoners</span><span class="soc-bar-s">Most people (the rest)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:87%;--k:5"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-dependent soc-unk" href="#rank-6" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Landless tenants</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:100%;--k:6"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-enslaved soc-unk" href="#rank-7" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Enslaved people</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li></ol><p class="soc-scale-note">By rank, the ladder is drawn as a pyramid. To scale, each bar is as wide as that rank&#8217;s share of the people; ranks no one has counted are dashed.</p></section>
